Taking $W_V$ to be zero directly gives $a_{r^\prime}=b_{s^\prime}$. In the following, we set $W_Q=I$, and for notational simplicity define \[ 	ilde k(x):=k(a_{r^\prime},x),\quad  orall\,x\in\mathbb{R}^d. \] The proof utilizes the following lemma:  egin{lemma}[Auxiliary lemma] Let $\{x_1,\cdots, x_p\}:=\{a_1,\cdots, a_r\}\cup \{b_1,\cdots, b_s\}$. Then, there exist $W_K\in\mathbb{R}^{d	imes d}$ and a permutation $\sigma\in S_p$ such that  egin{equation} \lim_{t	o\infty}  rac{	ilde k(tW_K x_{\sigma(j)})}{	ilde k(tW_K x_{\sigma(i)})}=\infty, 	ext{ for all } i<j. \end{equation} \end{lemma} Since $\{a_j\}_{j=1}^r$ are $r$ distinct tokens, by the auxiliary lemma, we may choose a $W_K$ (and reindex the sequences accordingly) so that after replacing $W_K$ by $tW_K$ the kernel values satisfy, for large $t>0$, \[ 	ilde k(tW_Ka_1)\ll 	ilde k(tW_Ka_2)\ll\cdots\ll 	ilde k(tW_Ka_r), \] and similarly \[ 	ilde k(tW_Kb_1)\ll 	ilde k(tW_Kb_2)\ll\cdots\ll 	ilde k(tW_Kb_s). \] Moreover, the lemma tells us that for any $a_j$ and $b_l$, either $a_j=b_l$ or one of $	ilde k(tW_ka_j)$ and $	ilde k(tW_kb_l)$ is dominated by the other in the limit $t	o\infty$. After subtracting $a_{r^\prime}$ from both sides in~\eqref{eq:lem_equal_atten}, we have  egin{equation}  rac{\sum_{j=1}^r 	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)a_j}{\sum_{j=1}^r 	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)} =  rac{\sum_{j=1}^s 	ilde k(tW_Kb_j)b_j}{\sum_{j=1}^s 	ilde k(tW_Kb_j)}. \end{equation} By a transformation, it gives  egin{equation} \sum_{j=1}^r\sum_{l=1}^s 	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)	ilde k(tW_Kb_l)(a_j-b_l)=0. \end{equation} Let $t	o \infty$, considering the dominate term $	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)	ilde k(tW_Kb_l)(a_r-b_s)$ gives $a_r=b_s$. Then, for all $q=0, 1,\cdots, \min\{r,s\}$, we then prove by induction that: $a_{r-k}=b_{s-k}$. Suppose we already have $a_{r-i}=b_{s-i}$, for $i=1,\cdots, q-1$. It then follows that  egin{equation}  egin{aligned} &\sum_{j=r-q+1}^r\sum_{l=s-q+1}^s 	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)	ilde k(tW_Kb_l)(a_j-b_l)\ &=\sum_{j=r-q+1}^r\sum_{l=r-q+1}^r 	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)	ilde k(tW_Ka_l)(a_j-a_l)=0, \quad 	ext{for all }t\in\mathbb R. \end{aligned} \end{equation}. Combining with~\eqref{eq:mean_eq2}, we have  egin{equation} (\sum_{j=1}^{r-q}\sum_{l=1}^{r-q}+\sum_{j=1}^{r-q}\sum_{l=s-q+1}^s+\sum_{j=r-q+1}^{r}\sum_{l=1}^{s-q}) \left(	ilde k(tW_Ka_j)	ilde k(tW_Kb_l)(a_j-b_l)ight)=0, \end{equation} where the leading term is  egin{equation} 	ilde k(tW_Ka_{r-q})	ilde k(tW_Kb_{s})(a_{r-q}-b_{s})+	ilde k(tW_Ka_{r})	ilde k(tW_Kb_{s-q})(a_{r}-b_{s-q}). \end{equation} Since $a_r=b_s$, $a_{r-q}
