We only need to prove the token distinguishability condition for two samples:  egin{itemize} \item For any $X$ and $Y$ that are in general positions and from different orbits of $G$(defined in~\eqref{eq:sparse_group}), there exists  egin{equation} g\in \mathcal G_m^\Phi:=\{(\operatorname{Id}+g_m)\circ \cdots \circ (\operatorname{Id}+g_1)\mid g_i\in\mathcal G_{\mathcal N_i}, 	ext{ for } i \in [m]\} \end{equation} such that the tokens of $g(X)$ and $g(Y)$ are all distinct. \end{itemize} We prove this claim by contradiction. Assume that, there exist $X$ and $Y$ that are in general positions and from different orbits of $G$, but for any $g\in \mathcal G_m^\Phi$, there exist indices $i,j\in [n]$ such that at least one token in $g(X)$ is identical to a token in $g(Y)$. Then, according to the analyticity, there exist indices $i_1$ and $i_2$ such that, $[g(X)]_{i_1}=[g(Y)]_{i_2}$ always hold. For a given $p_1\in [m]$, we first consider $g\in\mathrm{Id} +\mathcal G_{\mathcal N_{p_1}}$. Then, $[g(X)]_{i_1}=[g(Y)]_{i_2}$ gives:  egin{equation} [X]_{i_1}+ rac{\sum_{j\in\mathcal N_{p_1}(i_1)} k([W_QX]_{i_1},[W_KX]_j)[W_VX]_j}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal N_{p_1}(i_1)} k([W_QX]_{i_1},[W_KX]_j)}= [Y]_{i_2}+ rac{\sum_{l\in\mathcal N_{p_1}(i_2)} k([W_QY]_{i_2},[W_KY]_l)[W_VY]_l}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal N_{p_1}(i_2)} k([W_QY]_{i_2},[W_KY]_l)}, \end{equation} for any $W_Q, W_K, W_V\in\mathbb{R}^{d	imes d}$. According to \Cref{lem:equal_atten}, we can deduce that $|\mathcal N_{p_1}(i)|=|\mathcal N_{p_1}(j)|$, $[X]_{i_1}=[Y]_{i_2}$, and  egin{equation} \{[X]_q\mid q\in \mathcal N_{p_1}(i_1)\}=\{[Y]_q\mid q\in \mathcal N_{p_1}(i_2)\}. \end{equation} Therefore, choose any $q_1\in \mathcal N_{p_1}(i_1)$, we can find $q_2\in \mathcal N_{p_1}(i_2)$ such that $[X]_{q_1}=[Y]_{q_2}$. Now, we claim that for any $p_2<p_1$ and $g\in \mathrm{Id}+\mathcal G_{\mathcal N_{p_2}}$(a layer before the $p_1$-th layer) , $[g(X)]_{q_1}=[g(Y)]_{q_2}$. Otherwise, suppose there exists $g_1\in \mathrm{Id}+\mathcal G_{p_2}$ with $[g_1(X)]_{q_1}
eq [g_1(Y)]_{q_2}$. By scaling the $W_V$ matrix to be small enough, we can assume $g_1$ satisfies that  egin{equation} \|g_1(X)-X\|_2< rac{1}{2}\min_{i
