eq [g_2(g_1(Y))]_{q_2}$. Since $g_2\circ g_1\in (\mathrm{Id}+\mathcal G_{\mathcal N_{p_2}})\circ (\mathrm{Id}+\mathcal G_{\mathcal N_{p_1}})\subset \mathcal G_m^\Phi$ which can distinguish $[X]_{i_1}$ and $[Y]_{i_2}$, contradicting to our assumption. Hence, we have shown that for $p_2< p_1\le n$ and any $g \in \mathrm{Id}+\mathcal G_{\mathcal N_{p_2}}$, $[g(X)]_{q_1}=[g(Y)]_{q_2}$. Then, we can apply \Cref{lem:equal_atten} to $[X]_{q_1}$ and $[Y]_{q_2}$, and deduce that the set of tokens of $X$ with indices in $\mathcal N_{p_s}(q_1)$ are the same as those of $Y$ with indices in $\mathcal N_{p_3}(q_2)$. That is, the tokens where $[X]_{i_1}$ can attend to within two hops are the same as those where $[Y]_{i_2}$ can attend to within two hops. The above process can be repeated. Since we assume that $\Phi$ is connected within $m$ layers, we know that any indices in $[n]$ can be reached starting from $i_1$ and $i_2$ within $m$ hops. Finally, the above discussion can cover all indices in $[n]$. Since $X$ and $Y$ are in general positions, the correspondence between their tokens is unique. Finally, this results in a permutation $\sigma\in S_n$, such that:  egin{equation} [X]_i=[Y]_{\sigma(i)}. \end{equation} On the other hand, apply~\Cref{lem:equal_atten} again to eqch $\mathcal N_p$, $[X]_i$ and $[Y]_{\sigma(i)}$, we can deduce that  egin{equation} j\in\mathcal N_p(i) \Leftrightarrow \sigma(j)\in\mathcal N_p(\sigma(i)), 	ext{ for all } p\in [m]. \end{equation} By the definition of $\operatorname{Aut}(\mathcal N)$, this means that $\sigma$ belongs to each $\mathcal N_{ p}$, indicating that $\sigma \in G$. However, this contradicts to our assumption that $X$ and $Y$ are from different orbits of $\operatorname{Aut}(\Gamma)$, which completes the proof.

